WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.980
You grip a bar and look at it.

2
00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.300
Your eyes say 50 millimetres.

3
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:10.600
Your hand says 54.

4
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.720
These numbers are made up, but the question is not.

5
00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:19.520
A nervous system has to decide how much to trust each sense.

6
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:21.480
Which one do you believe?

7
00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:25.190
Each sense gives a noisy estimate.

8
00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:31.350
Suppose the noise in each is independent and Gaussian, the estimates are unbiased,

9
00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:34.370
and you have no prior preference for any height.

10
00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:39.410
Then the combination with the least variance is a weighted average.

11
00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:46.230
Each weight is the sense's precision, 1 over its variance, divided by the sum of the

12
00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:47.230
precisions.

13
00:00:47.633 --> 00:00:53.153
Take vision with a standard deviation of 2 millimetres and touch with 4.

14
00:00:53.873 --> 00:00:57.593
Their variances are 4 and 16 square millimetres.

15
00:00:58.513 --> 00:01:03.153
The weights come out at 0.8 for vision and 0.2 for touch.

16
00:01:04.053 --> 00:01:08.493
The combined estimate is 50.8 millimetres, much nearer to vision.

17
00:01:09.317 --> 00:01:13.397
The combined variance is 3.2 square millimetres.

18
00:01:14.237 --> 00:01:18.157
The standard deviation is about 1.79 millimetres.

19
00:01:18.857 --> 00:01:20.997
That is lower than either sense alone.

20
00:01:22.257 --> 00:01:25.997
Touch is the weaker cue, yet it still makes the estimate more precise.

21
00:01:27.377 --> 00:01:32.017
Under these assumptions, a second independent cue always lowers the variance.

22
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:38.060
Now blur the picture until vision has a standard deviation of 8 millimetres.

23
00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:44.040
The weights swap: touch gets 0.8 and vision 0.2.

24
00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.920
The estimate moves to 53.2 millimetres.

25
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:51.720
The same arithmetic that favoured vision now favours touch.

26
00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.920
Alais and Burr found a related pattern for sound and sight in humans: when they

27
00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.140
blurred the visual stimulus severely, sound captured the perceived location.

28
00:02:03.833 --> 00:02:09.733
Ernst and Banks tested this with four human observers, who judged bar height by

29
00:02:09.733 --> 00:02:14.093
sight and by touch while noise was added to the visual stimulus.

30
00:02:14.813 --> 00:02:18.573
A maximum-likelihood model closely resembled their judgements.

31
00:02:19.353 --> 00:02:23.373
That is one task, four people, and a behavioural fit.

32
00:02:23.993 --> 00:02:27.053
It does not show where in the brain the weighting happens.

33
00:02:27.893 --> 00:02:33.293
Keep the rule: which sense wins follows its reliability in the task at hand.
